# General Standards

- Keep changes focused and minimal for the requested task.
- Do not remove or rewrite unrelated existing behavior.
- Prefer readable, maintainable code over clever shortcuts.
- Add or update tests for behavior changes when practical.
- Avoid destructive git commands unless explicitly requested.
- Do not commit secrets or credentials.
- Validate with lint/tests after meaningful edits when available.
- If requirements are unclear, ask a concise clarifying question first.

- Always create small, focused components.
- Each component should have a single responsibility.
- Avoid large components with multiple concerns.

- If a component is reused in multiple places, move it to `common/` folder.
- Reusable components must be generic and not tied to specific business logic.

- Keep components under ~100 lines when possible.
- Split complex UI into smaller subcomponents.

- Use clear and descriptive names for components.
- Avoid duplication — reuse existing components from `common/` if available.

# Frontend Project Conventions

- Do not use inline styles in React components (no `style={{ ... }}`).
- Do not use TypeScript `eslint-disable` comments to bypass linting.
- Do not use `any` in TypeScript code.
- Do not use `interface`; use `type` aliases instead.
- Place each component in its own folder named after the component.
- Use this component structure:
  - `ComponentName/index.tsx`
  - `ComponentName/style.(css|scss|module.css)` for styles
  - `ComponentName/const.(ts|tsx)` for constants
  - `ComponentName/utils.(ts|tsx)` for helpers

  # Ant Design Styling Rules

- Always wrap the app with Ant Design ConfigProvider.
- Use ConfigProvider theme to override and reset styles globally.

- Do NOT use inline styles for Ant Design components.
- Do NOT override styles locally unless absolutely necessary.

- All style customization must go through ConfigProvider theme tokens.

- Use design tokens (colorPrimary, borderRadius, fontSize, etc.) instead of hardcoded values.

- Keep styling consistent by relying on global theme configuration.
